  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Periods of valley fog and low clouds (stratus), especially in\n   western Montana.\n\n-  A weather system will bring showers, light rain or snow, and\n   high-elevation snow on Sunday.\n\n-  High pressure next week bringing more valley inversions.\n\nAnother weak disturbance will be moving through the Northern\nRockies this afternoon into the evening. This feature should help\nlift and break up some of the fog and low stratus across the\nregion, but it will not complete break up the valley inversions.\nLater this evening and overnight, freezing fog and stratus will\nredevelop in the typical locations. \n\nA southerly wind is expected to develop by Saturday afternoon\nwhich will help mix out some of the valleys with significant\nwarming, especially across portions of north central Idaho, Lemhi\ncounty, southwest Montana, Bitterroot and Mission valleys. While\nthe Missoula valley and areas across northwest Montana will have a\ndifficult time breaking out of the inversions which will keep\ntemperatures around 10 degrees cooler than previously forecasted.\n\nA cold front is expected to push across the region on Sunday which\nwill bring some scattered showers to northwest Montana. The\nbiggest impact from this front will be valley inversions finally\nbreaking up and Sunday potentially being the warmest day. This\nfront will bring gusty winds to the region with widespread gusts\nof 20 to 30 mph by Sunday afternoon. The higher terrain along the\nContinental Divide, especially around Glacier NP, should\nanticipate wind gusts over 40 mph. \n\nAnother subtle disturbance appears to move through the Northern\nRockies on Tuesday keeping valley inversions from fully developing\nalong with the potential for some light showers across northwest\nMontana. Then the long term models are leaning towards a\nsignificant ridge of high pressure over the region Wednesday\nthrough the end of the work week. This ridge has a high potential\nof allowing strong valley inversions to redevelop across the\nregion. These inversions will cause widespread fog and low stratus\nand keep temperatures in the valleys from reaching their full\npotential.",
